  <div class="callout warn"><strong>No current GovWait estimate.</strong> A historical processing time, a forum timeline or the wait for a different visitor or work visa is not a valid present-day Critical Purpose Visitor Visa estimate.</div>

  <h2>What the Critical Purpose Visitor Visa was</h2>
  <p>INZ’s official July 2022 announcement says the border-exception regime and Critical Purpose Visitor Visas were created in response to border restrictions introduced in 2020. The historical instructions describe a two-step path: a person or supporting organization first expressed interest in travelling, and INZ could then invite the person to apply for a visa or variation of conditions if the critical-purpose requirements appeared likely to be met.</p>
  <p>Those instructions are now archived. They are useful for understanding an old application or visa, but they are not a current invitation to apply.</p>

  <h2>When the border-exception route closed</h2>
  <p>INZ announced that all main border-exception categories and class exceptions would close on <strong>July 31, 2022</strong>. Primary-sector class exceptions were scheduled to close on <strong>September 1, 2022</strong>, with limited transitional arrangements for seafood-processing workers and deep-sea fishers. The announcement said requests received before the applicable deadline would continue to be considered and, if approved, a person could still be invited to apply.</p>
  <p>That distinction matters: the deadline closed new border-exception requests, but it did not mean every request or invited visa application already in progress received a decision on the same day. INZ did not publish one current universal completion time for all of those historical cases.</p>

  <h2>How to check an older application</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Use the INZ account tied to the application.</strong> INZ says the account can show whether more information is needed and whatever progress information is available.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch the original contact channels.</strong> INZ may phone, email or post an account notification when it needs supporting information.</li>
    <li><strong>Use INZ’s official contact route when appropriate.</strong> Its current status guidance says applicants can contact INZ when an application is taking longer than the normal timeframe for that visa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>GovWait cannot see a private application, determine whether an old invitation remains usable, or infer a queue position. Do not post an application number, passport details or other personal information here.</p>

  <h2>Do not confuse it with current visa names</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/new-zealand/specific-purpose-work-visa/">Specific Purpose Work Visa</a> is a current, separate route for qualifying work tied to a specific purpose or event. Its eligibility evidence, conditions and processing figures do not retroactively describe the COVID-era Critical Purpose Visitor Visa.</p>
  <p>INZ’s phase-out announcement also referred workers to the then-new Accredited Employer Work Visa as the border reopened. That historical transition is not a promise that an AEWV—or any other current visa—is suitable for a particular person today. Current eligibility must be checked with INZ or a licensed adviser.</p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <h3>Can I submit a new Critical Purpose Visitor Visa application?</h3>
  <p>The general COVID border-exception pathway is no longer open. INZ’s official announcement closed the main exception requests in July 2022 and the primary-sector exceptions in September 2022, subject to the limited transitions described above.</p>
  <h3>How long did a Critical Purpose Visitor Visa take?</h3>
  <p>There is no single current official number GovWait can responsibly publish. Timing depended on the historical exception category, the expression-of-interest and invitation steps, the visa application, and the facts of the case.</p>
  <h3>Is a Specific Purpose Work Visa the same visa?</h3>
  <p>No. It is a current work route with its own purpose-or-event requirements. Similar wording does not make the categories or their processing times interchangeable.</p>

